  "holistic_F_N": 2,
  "holistic_F_H": -1,
  "section_notes": "The final S2 keeps the 5% limit but has no sales stop and an open-ended, renewable mitigation plan, so the Hearth 10 can remain on sale, and per-model rates go to customers but not the public. S3 sets new-unit shut-off at 24 months (beyond the 12-month acceptable range) and a 72-month retrofit, which gives Northwind a longer transition. S5 keeps the mandatory 8/100k recall with no transition, which exposes the Ember 8 (9.9/100k), while S1 is a strong 24-hour reporting regime with a confirmed-incidents scope for non-serious events. The 'numeric_rates' coding for S2 customer results assumes 'results' means numeric per-model failure rates, which the text does not state explicitly."
